WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 6: A Different Type of Story.

2
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.000
In this unit you will explore the many
different types of stories that writers create.

3
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.560
Every story belongs to a particular genre,
which is the French word for type or kind.

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:21.300
By the end of this unit you will understand
several genres, recognise their special features,

5
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.640
and be able to choose the right genre for your own writing.

6
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:31.860
You will also strengthen your grammar by learning how to
connect ideas smoothly using connectives and conjunctions.

7
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.640
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

8
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.660
By the end of this unit,

9
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.360
you will be able to summarise the central ideas and
important details in a different type of story texts.

10
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.520
you will also support inferences and
comparisons with relevant text evidence.

11
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.020
you will also use grade 5 vocabulary
accurately in discussion and writing.

12
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.320
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

13
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:08.080
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: unrest,
uneasy, unexpected, unsuccessful, unfamiliar, uncertain.

14
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.740
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

15
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.320
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

16
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:25.800
The reading sequence includes A Different Type of Story:
source text 1 and A Different Type of Story: source text 2.

17
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.140
Read actively.

18
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.860
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

19
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.960
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

20
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.720
The first language focus is What
Are Connectives and Conjunctions?.

21
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.280
A connective is any word or phrase
that links ideas in your writing.

22
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.660
A conjunction is a specific type of connective that
joins words, phrases, or clauses within a sentence.

23
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.140
People often use the two words to mean the same thing.

24
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:56.480
Why do they matter?

25
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.340
Without connectives, your writing would
be a list of short, choppy sentences.

26
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.660
Connectives make your ideas flow and
help the reader follow your thinking.

27
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.060
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

28
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.660
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Genre Detective Game.

29
00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.180
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

30
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.840
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

31
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.300
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

32
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:30.800
You are ready to begin.

33
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:37.060
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

34
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.760
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

35
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:45.680
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
